{"vote":"right","reasoning":"The right video delivers the brief's beats more clearly. It shows "You ask", then "It edits the file", then "It renders the video", so the story runs ask, edit, result. The terminal is shown at a readable size. The result card is labelled as the render output, which makes it read as the outcome of the session. The claim "Video is now a file your agent can edit." is short and plain, and only the word "file" carries the accent colour, which fits the brief. It holds for two frames, so it stays on screen long enough to read twice. The ending lockup has a tagline, "The video editor your agent drives."

The left video has weaker spots. It shows the claim twice, once with "change" in orange and once without, and then repeats it a third time under the final logo, so the copy is redundant. Its terminal shots are cramped. It has a grey, washed-out transition frame, which looks like a mid-fade defect. Its result card has no explicit link back to the work just shown beyond "Out comes the video."

The right video's small text, such as the dense terminal output and the "out/hello-text.mp4" caption, is small at phone size. The hook's blinking cursor block is a minor oddity. These flaws are smaller than the left video's."}

