{"vote":"left","reasoning":"Left covers more of the brief. It shows the real terminal session in a framed card, with a visible diff in green. Its result still is clearly labelled "then renders the result." and the subtitle is legible, with an accent underline. The claim is "Video becomes a file your agent can edit", with only the word "file" in the accent colour. It ends on a clean brand lockup with a tagline. Left has weaknesses. The terminal text is small and cramped. One result frame looks greyed out, probably mid-transition. The hook has no accent colour.

Right has a strong hook, "Your AI agent edits video like code", with the accent on one word. But its proof frames are weaker. The terminal is cropped awkwardly, with cut-off text on the left edge. The diff is tiny and hard to read, and the result is a plain dark box with a thin border. The result does not look like the rendered video, so it reads less clearly as an outcome. The two near-identical claim frames waste screen time. The final lockup is fine.

Left's proof and result are more convincing and better presented, so I prefer Left."}

